FEX: deliver a pending signal on sigsuspend and sigtimedwait (SteamARM)

A signal that reaches FEX while the guest masks it (the emulated mask is
updated before the host mask in GuestSigProcMask, so a signal can land in
between; a guest handler also runs with a host mask narrower than the
guest's) is taken from the host and recorded only in PendingSignals, to be
re-raised when the guest unmasks it. GuestSigProcMask does that
(CheckForPendingSignals); GuestSigSuspend only checked after the host
sigsuspend returned, and GuestSigTimedWait never did. So a guest that
blocks SIGCHLD, forks, and waits in sigsuspend with SIGCHLD unmasked slept
forever when its SIGCHLD had come in that window. MEASURED under SteamARM
(x86-64 Android's mksh running `x=$(toybox echo x); y=$(toybox seq 3 |
toybox wc -l)` under FEX, an instrumented FEX's log: "SIGCHLD -> pending
10000", then "sigsuspend set=80000000 pend=10000" and no wake): 3 hangs in
300 runs with this alone missing (and SteamARM's runtime fix for signals
XNU strands on its main thread in place), 0 in 300 with it.
benchmarks/stage28-android-reliability.txt.

Now GuestSigSuspend raises each pending signal the suspend mask lets
through again (tgkill to itself) with the host mask blocking it, so the
host sigsuspend finds it pending, delivers it and returns, as Linux's does;
the host mask is put back afterwards. GuestSigTimedWait does the same for
pending signals of its set, so the host rt_sigtimedwait can take them.

diff --git a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SignalDelegator.cpp b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SignalDelegator.cpp
--- a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SignalDelegator.cpp
+++ b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SignalDelegator.cpp
@@ -1282,6 +1282,29 @@
 
   // Backup the mask
   Thread->SignalInfo.PreviousSuspendMask = Thread->SignalInfo.CurrentSignalMask;
+
+  // A signal that arrived while the guest had it masked -- in GuestSigProcMask
+  // between the emulated mask and the host mask being updated, or while a
+  // guest handler ran with a host mask narrower than the guest's -- was taken
+  // from the host already and is only recorded in PendingSignals. If the
+  // suspend mask lets it through, Linux's sigsuspend delivers it and returns
+  // at once; the host sigsuspend below cannot see it and would sleep forever
+  // (MEASURED under SteamARM: mksh's `x=$(toybox ...)` waited for a SIGCHLD
+  // that sat in PendingSignals). Raise each such signal again with the host
+  // mask blocking it, so the host sigsuspend finds it pending and delivers it.
+  // (SteamARM's tests/android/run.sh looks for this string in the binary.)
+  [[maybe_unused]] static const char LxrtSigsuspendPending[] __attribute__((used, retain)) = "lxrt-sigsuspend-pending";
+  const uint64_t Deliverable = Thread->SignalInfo.PendingSignals & ~(*set & IgnoredSignalsMask);
+  uint64_t HostMaskBefore {};
+  if (Deliverable) {
+    ::syscall(SYS_rt_sigprocmask, SIG_BLOCK, &Deliverable, &HostMaskBefore, 8);
+    for (int32_t i = 0; i < MAX_SIGNALS; ++i) {
+      if (Deliverable & (1ULL << i)) {
+        FHU::Syscalls::tgkill(Thread->ThreadInfo.PID, Thread->ThreadInfo.TID, i + 1);
+      }
+    }
+  }
+
   // Set the new mask
   Thread->SignalInfo.CurrentSignalMask.Val = *set & IgnoredSignalsMask;
   sigset_t HostSet {};
@@ -1302,6 +1325,10 @@
   // Spin this in a loop until we aren't sigsuspended
   // This can happen in the case that the guest has sent signal that we can't block
   uint64_t Result = sigsuspend(&HostSet);
+  const int SuspendErrno = errno;
+  if (Deliverable) {
+    ::syscall(SYS_rt_sigprocmask, SIG_SETMASK, &HostMaskBefore, nullptr, 8);
+  }
 
   // Restore Previous signal mask we are emulating
   // XXX: Might be unsafe if the signal handler adjusted the thread's signal mask
@@ -1311,13 +1338,33 @@
 
   CheckForPendingSignals(Thread);
 
-  return Result == -1 ? -errno : Result;
+  return Result == -1 ? -SuspendErrno : Result;
 }
 
-uint64_t SignalDelegator::GuestSigTimedWait(uint64_t* set, siginfo_t* info, const struct timespec* timeout, size_t sigsetsize) {
+uint64_t SignalDelegator::GuestSigTimedWait(FEX::HLE::ThreadStateObject* Thread, uint64_t* set, siginfo_t* info,
+                                            const struct timespec* timeout, size_t sigsetsize) {
+  // The same for a wait: a signal of the set the host already handed to FEX
+  // while the guest masked it is only in PendingSignals, invisible to the host
+  // rt_sigtimedwait. Raise it again, blocked on the host, for the wait to take.
+  const uint64_t Held = sigsetsize == 8 && set ? (Thread->SignalInfo.PendingSignals & *set) : 0;
+  uint64_t HostMaskBefore {};
+  if (Held) {
+    ::syscall(SYS_rt_sigprocmask, SIG_BLOCK, &Held, &HostMaskBefore, 8);
+    Thread->SignalInfo.PendingSignals &= ~Held;
+    for (int32_t i = 0; i < MAX_SIGNALS; ++i) {
+      if (Held & (1ULL << i)) {
+        FHU::Syscalls::tgkill(Thread->ThreadInfo.PID, Thread->ThreadInfo.TID, i + 1);
+      }
+    }
+  }
+
   uint64_t Result = ::syscall(SYS_rt_sigtimedwait, set, info, timeout, sigsetsize);
+  const int WaitErrno = errno;
+  if (Held) {
+    ::syscall(SYS_rt_sigprocmask, SIG_SETMASK, &HostMaskBefore, nullptr, 8);
+  }
 
-  return Result == -1 ? -errno : Result;
+  return Result == -1 ? -WaitErrno : Result;
 }
 
